If you are talking about interface - it is via `@Inject` and `InjectionToken`. 
To provide I am using `provideResultProcessor` like below.
All is provided in component scope, which is a base in a route.
```ts

export const RESULT_PROCESSOR_TOKEN = new InjectionToken("result_processor");

export interface RouteResultProcessor {
  readonly priority: number

  onNewResult(result: RouteResultsAccess): RouteResultsAccess
}

export function provideResultProcessor(type) {
  return {
    provide: RESULT_PROCESSOR_TOKEN,
    useClass: type,
    multi: true
  }
}
```

PS: those are later sorted via priority - and as I see when I make a `slice` on that array and sort it - it works then without exception.
BUT that `this` is strange anyway.

Can you post an example where the `provideResultProcessor` is used? I'm trying to put this into a unit test, but it's behaving as expected. Here's what I'm doing:

```
it('should fail', () => {
  const destroyCalls: any[] = [];
  const SERVICES = new InjectionToken<any>('SERVICES');

  @Injectable()
  class Service0 {
  }

  @Injectable()
  class Service1 {
    ngOnDestroy() {
      destroyCalls.push(this);
    }
  }

  @Injectable()
  class Service2 {
  }

  @Injectable()
  class Service3 {
    ngOnDestroy() {
      destroyCalls.push(this);
    }
  }

  @Injectable()
  class Service4 {
  }

  @Component({
    template: '<div></div>',
    providers: [
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service0, multi: true},
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service1, multi: true},
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service2, multi: true},
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service3, multi: true},
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service4, multi: true},
    ]
  })
  class App {
    constructor(@Inject(SERVICES) s: any) {}
  }

  TestBed.configureTestingModule({declarations: [App]});
  const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(App);
  fixture.detectChanges();
  fixture.destroy();

  // Logs [ Service1 {}, Service3 {} ] as expected.
  console.log(destroyCalls);
});
```

I took a better look and it and it seems like this uncovered an issue with `multi` providers that has been there since ViewEngine. If the array returned from a `multi` provider is mutated, it'll propagate both in our internal data structures, causing issues like `ngOnDestroy` getting called with the wrong context, as well as any other places that inject the same token. You probably didn't see it, because we weren't calling `ngOnDestroy` for `multi` providers. 

Here's a test that shows the issue. Two instances of the same directive request an injection token and get the providers in a different order:

```it('', () => {
  const instances: any[] = [];
  const SERVICES = new InjectionToken<any>('SERVICES');

  @Injectable()
  class Service0 {
  }

  @Injectable()
  class Service1 {
  }

  @Directive({selector: '[dir]'})
  class Dir {
    constructor(@Inject(SERVICES) s: any[]) {
      instances.push(s.slice());
      s.reverse();
    }
  }

  @Component({
    template: '<div dir></div><div dir></div>',
    providers: [
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service0, multi: true},
      {provide: SERVICES, useClass: Service1, multi: true},
    ]
  })
  class App {
  }

  TestBed.configureTestingModule({declarations: [App, Dir]});
  const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(App);
  fixture.detectChanges();

  // Logs [[Service0, Service1], [Service1, Sevice0]]
  console.log(instances);
});
```
